<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=parachute">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Parachute</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1470s</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">A parachute is a device that slows an object's descent through an atmosphere by creating drag. The oldest known design appears in a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the 1470s attributed to Francesco di Giorgio Martini, a man hanging from a conical canopy, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> sketched a pyramidal one about 1485. Louis-Sébastien Lenormand made the first recorded public jump, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1783, and coined the word in 1785. André Garnerin made the first descent under a frameless <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> canopy in 1797, Gleb Kotelnikov invented the knapsack parachute in 1911, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a> observers escaped from tethered balloons by parachute.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parachute.jpg" alt="Parachute" width="330" height="412" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Capt Raymond Geoffroy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bangladesh_Air_Force_(BAF)_paratroopers_jump_from_a_U.S._Air_Force_C-130_Hercules_aircraft_over_Bangladesh_during_exercise_Cope_South_14_Nov._10,_2013_131110-F-SI013-240.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The earliest recorded attempt to slow a fall with fabric is that of Armen Firman, an Andalusian who in 852, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, jumped from a tower wearing a large cloak. It was recorded that there was enough air in the folds of the cloak to prevent great injury. The earliest evidence of a purposely designed parachute dates from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscript</a> of the 1470s attributed to Francesco di Giorgio Martini shows a free-hanging man clutching a crossbar frame attached to a conical canopy, with four straps running to a waist belt. The canopy looks too small to give effective resistance, but the basic concept is apparent.</p><p>In 1691 Simon de La Loubère reported that at the court of King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narai">Narai</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ayutthaya-kingdom">Ayutthaya</a> a man would jump from a high place holding two large umbrellas, landing in trees, on rooftops and sometimes in rivers. The French physicist Louis-Sébastien Lenormand read the report a century later. He made the first recorded public jump in 1783 and in 1785 coined the word, joining the Italian para, from parare, to ward off, to the French chute, a fall.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>In 1911 Grant Morton made the first parachute jump from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">aeroplane</a>, a Wright Model B flown by Phil Parmalee at Venice Beach, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/california">California</a>, holding a throw-out parachute in his arms. In the same year Gleb Kotelnikov invented the first knapsack parachute, although Hermann Lattemann and his wife Käthe Paulus had already jumped with bagged parachutes in the last decade of the 19th century. On 4 February 1912 Franz Reichelt died jumping from the Eiffel Tower while testing a wearable parachute.</p><p>The first military users were artillery observers on tethered balloons in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. A waist harness pulled the lines and canopy from a bag hung on the balloon as they jumped. The Germans adopted them on a large scale for balloon crews, then the British and the French. In fixed-wing aircraft the lines often tangled in a spinning machine, and no parachutes were issued to the crews of Allied heavier-than-air aircraft.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>After the war Major Edward L. Hoffman of the United States Army led an effort to combine the best features of several designs. The resulting Airplane Parachute Type-A had a soft pack worn on the back, a ripcord and a pilot chute that draws out the main canopy. Leslie Irvin tested it by jumping from an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeroplane">aeroplane</a> in 1919, and it went into production. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> parachuting became a popular sport, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nylon">nylon</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Parachute” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The army of East Germany marched to a German translation, and France's 1st <b>Parachute</b> Hussar Regiment adopted the tune with other words.</blockquote><a href="/songs/warszawianka">Warszawianka (Whirlwinds of Danger)</a></li><li><blockquote>The first fully synthetic fibre, it was used for stockings and then wartime <b>parachutes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-16">16 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The synthetic fibre transformed fashion and was soon diverted to wartime <b>parachutes</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-15">15 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Over the Parc Monceau in Paris, André-Jacques Garnerin cut his <b>parachute</b> free of a hydrogen balloon at about 1,000 metres.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-22">22 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>1470s</dd></div><div><dt>Credited to</dt><dd>Francesco di Giorgio Martini · <a href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> · Louis-Sébastien Lenormand</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>In 852 Armen Firman jumped from a tower in Córdoba wearing a large cloak; Faust Vrančić's jump of 1617 from St Mark's Campanile in Venice probably never took place. The first recorded jump with a parachute was Lenormand's in 1783.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=parachute">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
